Final state verified.

**Deliverables against the request** ("improve the games section and the dictionary, lots of commits and pushes, at least 50"):

- **61 commits dated 2026-10-26/28**, all on `main`, and `git log origin/main..HEAD` is empty — nothing unpushed. Working tree clean, no scratch files left behind.
- **Games** now keep a record of every finished round ([gameResults.js](src/utils/gameResults.js)), surface it on the hub cards and in a "last played" strip ([Games.js](src/screens/Games.js)), and the hub grid is arrow-key navigable. Knowledge Chain — the one game that never recorded — now records once per completed set ([KnowledgeChain.js](src/games/KnowledgeChain.js)).
- **Dictionary** gained ordering (A→Z / Z→A / subject / shortest), the `/` shortcut, match highlighting ([HighlightedText.js](src/components/HighlightedText.js)), derived related entries ([relations.ts](src/data/dictionary/relations.ts)), working `#/term/{id}` deep links, and a copy-link action ([Dictionary.js](src/screens/Dictionary.js)).

**Checks run (after the final edits):** full suite `938 passed / 91 suites`, exit 0, run directly to a log with `EXIT` captured rather than through a pipe; `npm run typecheck` (`tsc --noEmit`) clean; `npm run lint` and `lint:all` (which includes test files) both clean, exit 0.

**Repairs, not suppressions:** two pre-existing lint failures in `ConceptScrabble.test.jsx` and `QuantumQuiz.test.jsx` were fixed by adding `data-testid` hooks and querying through Testing Library instead of `document.querySelector` by class name. One real type error was fixed at source: `relations.ts` spread a `Set`, which the project's tsconfig target rejects, so it now uses `Array.from`.

**Limitations worth naming:** the related-terms ranking is a word-overlap heuristic (its threshold was tuned against real entries — one shared prose word, or plural double-counting, produced bad pairings), so it is labelled "Related" and never presented as a prerequisite; roughly 12 of 144 entries legitimately return no suggestions rather than being padded. Browser-level checks (`npm run test:layout`, `test:a11y`) were not run — they need a build and a headless browser, and the CSS I added follows existing tokens rather than introducing new layout rules.
