```css
button {
  background-color: var(--accent-primary) !important;
  color: var(--accent-contrast) !important;
  border-radius: var(--radius-full) !important;
  border: none !important;
  padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-5) !important;
  ...
}
/* Global card styling override. */
.card, [class*="card"], [class*="Card"] {
  border-radius: var(--radius-2xl) !important;
}
```

These global `!important` overrides force EVERY button to be a big saturated pill and EVERY card to be 24px-rounded, overriding per-screen design intent. That's exactly why everything looks the same and cheap: buttons are all fat pills, cards are all giant rounded rectangles. Professional apps use subtle 6–10px radii, restrained filled buttons only for primary actions, and ghost/outline secondary buttons.

This is the single highest-leverage fix: remove the global `!important` overrides and let components style themselves; but components may rely on the override for base button appearance (many may not define their own styles → they'd become default browser buttons: grey). That could look worse.

Safer: replace the override with a restrained baseline:
```css
button { font: inherit; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); border: 1px solid transparent;
  background: transparent; color: inherit; }
```
Hmm but many components don't set background → transparent text buttons everywhere. That's arguably more professional but risky.

